  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0533E9">Lemma <span data-tag="0533E9">8.2.8.3</span>.</a> Let ${\mathbf A}$ be a presentable category, and $\calC $ a small category. Let $S_0$ be a (small) set of morphisms of ${\mathbf A}$, and let $\overline{S}_0$ be the weakly saturated class of morphisms generated by $S_0$. Let $\widetilde{S}$ be the collection of all morphisms $F \rightarrow G$ in $\Fun (\calC , {\mathbf A})$ with the following property: for every $C \in \calC $, the map $F(C) \rightarrow G(C)$ belongs to $\overline{S}_0$. Then there exists a $($small$)$ set of morphisms $S$ of $\Fun (\calC , {\mathbf A})$ which generates $\widetilde{S}$ as a weakly saturated class of morphisms. </p>
